    "https://jobs.hku.hk/cw/en/job/537266/nurse-holding-the-functional-title-of-research-nurse": {
      "title": "HKU Careers",
      "description": "HKU Careers - Career Opportunities Skip to content HKU Home Home Start main Content Career Opportunities Home Explore Careers at HKU Career Opportunities Applicant login Job alert Internal openings Job Search e.g. Professor Refine Search Work type Full-time 1 Categories Executive / Technical / Support 1 Faculty Li Ka Shing Faculty of Medicine 1 Nurse (holding the functional title of Research Nurse) The University of Hong Kong Apply now Ref.: 537266 Work type: Full-time Department: School of Nursing (20800) Categories: Executive / Technical / Support Hong Kong Nurse (holding the functional title of Research Nurse) in the School of Nursing (Ref.: 537266) (to commence as soon as possible, on a one-year temporary basis or two-year fixed term basis, with the possibility of renewal subject to funding availability and satisfactory performance) Applicants should possess a Bachelor’s degree in Nursing and be registered with the Nursing Council of Hong Kong.  Relevant experience and/or interest in clinical research, project coordination, or working in NGOs or with individuals with disabilities would be highly preferred.  They should demonstrate strong clinical skills in health assessment, chronic disease management, and functional rehabilitation, along with excellent interpersonal and communication abilities to work effectively in multidisciplinary teams.  Fluency in both written and spoken English and Chinese (including Cantonese) is required.  Familiarity with digital health tools (e.g. wearable devices) and IT systems are considered advantages. The appointee will work for the “Jockey Club Health Programme for Adult Persons with Disabilities” under the supervision of Professor Kelvin Wang.  He/She will assist in liaising with internal and external parties to implement the project, provide direct nursing care and health monitoring for participants, coordinate and support health-related interventions, assist in care planning, conduct health education, an