    "https://jobs.hku.hk/cw/en/job/537045/tenuretrack-assistant-professor-several-posts": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor (several posts)",
      "description": "HKU Careers - Career Opportunities Skip to content HKU Home Home Start main Content Career Opportunities Home Explore Careers at HKU Career Opportunities Applicant login Job alert Internal openings Job Search e.g. Professor Refine Search Work type Full-time 1 Categories Professoriate Staff 1 Faculty Li Ka Shing Faculty of Medicine 1 Tenure-Track Assistant Professor (several posts) The University of Hong Kong Apply now Ref.: 537045 Work type: Full-time Department: Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, School of Clinical Medicine (21000) Categories: Professoriate Staff Hong Kong Applications are invited for appointment as Tenure-Track Assistant Professor (several posts) in the Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, School of Clinical Medicine (Ref.: 537045), to commence as soon as possible, on a three-year fixed term basis, with the possibility of renewal and consideration for tenure before the expiry of a second three-year fixed-term contract, subject to satisfactory performance. Applicants should possess: (i) a Ph.D. degree in Medicine, Surgery, Biomedical Engineering, Health Science, or a related discipline; (ii) a strong track record of high-quality research demonstrated by publication record and external grants; (iii) evidence of international recognition; (iv) established cross-disciplinary collaborative ties both locally and internationally; and (iv) an outstanding record of teaching, mentoring and supervising research postgraduate students. The appointees are expected to participate in the planning and delivery of undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, supervise research staff and research postgraduate students, conduct high-impact scholarly research in areas such as musculoskeletal science, stem cells and regenerative medicine, and contribute to service / administration at the Department and Faculty levels. The ideal candidates should be innovative and proactive, possess excellent problem-solving skill, logical thinking and strong data sensitivity.  Additionally, they will play a key role in advancing the department’s translational research and fostering strategic partnerships. A highly competitive salary commensurate with qualifications and experience will be offered, in addition to annual leave and medical benefits.  At current rates, salaries tax does not exceed 15% of gross income.  The appointment will attract a contract-end gratuity and University contribution to a retirement benefits scheme, totalling up to 15% of basic salary.  Housing benefits will also be provided as applicable. The University only accepts online applications for the above posts.  Applicants should apply online and upload an up-to-date C.V. with contact information of at least three referees, and statements of teaching and research.  Review of applications will start from October 19, 2026 and continue until November 19, 2026 , or until the posts are filled, whichever is earlier. Advertised: Aug 20, 2026 (HK Time) Applications close: Nov 19, 2026 (HK Time) Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Great, we can send you jobs like this, if this is your first time signing up, please check your inbox to confirm your subscription. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Ref. Posting Title Department Closing Date 537045 Tenure-Track Assistant Professor (several posts) Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, School of Clinical Medicine (21000) Nov 19, 2026 Featured jobs Current opportunities Ref. Posting Title Department Closing Date 537045 Tenure-Track Assistant Professor (several posts) Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, School of Clinical Medicine (21000) Nov 19, 2026 Powered by PageUp Copyright © 2026\n                Human Resources Office,\n                The University of Hong Kong. All Rights Reserved."
